{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-342306" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n({x}^{2}-4){y}^{″}+5x{y}^{′}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The functions $P(x)=\\frac{5x}{{x}^{2}-4}$ and $Q(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{{x}^{2}-4}$ are analytic about $x=2.$\n- The functions $(x-2)P(x)$ and $(x-2)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=2.$\n- The point $x=2$ is an **irregular** singular point of the differential equation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065872/state.json" "file_sha256" "3d1e52a9f2d0b71be6459449c00f09b0f6cf4f1378677164ef8d0ba4678f2945"} {"question" "q-342306" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A singular point $x=a$ of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nis regular if both $(x-a)P(x)$ and $(x-a)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, the singular point is irregular.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $({x}^{2}-4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{2}-4){y}^{″}+5x{y}^{′}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{5x}{{x}^{2}-4}{y}^{′}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{{x}^{2}-4}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{5x}{{x}^{2}-4}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{{x}^{2}-4}.\n$$\n\nLet's now check each statement in turn.\n\n- Since the denominators of both $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ vanish at $x=2,$ they are *not* analytic about $x=2.$ So, statement I is false.\n\n- The function\n\n$$\n(x-2)P(x)=(x-2)\\cdot \\frac{5x}{{x}^{2}-4}=\\frac{5x}{x+2}\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=2,$ and the function\n\n$$\n(x-2)^{2}Q(x)=(x-2)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{{x}^{2}-4}=\\frac{(x-2)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{x+2}\n$$\n\nis also analytic about $x=2.$ Therefore, statement II is true.\n\n- Since statement I is false, $x=2$ is a singular point. Also, since statement II is true, it follows that $x=2$ is a **regular** singular point. Thus, statement III is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065872/state.json" "file_sha256" "3d1e52a9f2d0b71be6459449c00f09b0f6cf4f1378677164ef8d0ba4678f2945"} {"question" "q-342306" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065872/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e6600d27b8914742645eb0dde196ab79c45c242c300896e379514617f12f5a0c"} {"question" "q-342306" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065872/state.json" "file_sha256" "3d1e52a9f2d0b71be6459449c00f09b0f6cf4f1378677164ef8d0ba4678f2945"} {"question" "q-342306" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II  only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065872/state.json" "file_sha256" "3d1e52a9f2d0b71be6459449c00f09b0f6cf4f1378677164ef8d0ba4678f2945"} {"question" "q-342306" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II  only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065872/state.json" "file_sha256" "3d1e52a9f2d0b71be6459449c00f09b0f6cf4f1378677164ef8d0ba4678f2945"} {"question" "q-342307" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n(x+2){y}^{″}+{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{2}}y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The functions $P(x)=\\frac{1}{x+2}$ and $Q(x)=\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{3}}$ are analytic about $x=-2.$\n- The functions $(x+2)P(x)$ and $(x+2)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=-2.$\n- The point $x=-2$ is an **irregular** singular point of the differential equation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065872/state.json" "file_sha256" "3d1e52a9f2d0b71be6459449c00f09b0f6cf4f1378677164ef8d0ba4678f2945"} {"question" "q-342307" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A singular point $a$ of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nis regular if both $(x-a)P(x)$ and $(x-a)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, the singular point is irregular.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $(x+2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2){y}^{″}+{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{2}}y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{x+2}{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{3}}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{1}{x+2}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{3}}.\n$$\n\nLet's now check each statement in turn.\n\n- Since the denominators of both $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ vanish at $x=-2,$ they are *not* analytic about $x=-2.$ So, statement I is false.\n\n- The function\n\n$$\n(x+2)P(x)=(x+2)\\frac{1}{x+2}=1\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=-2,$ while the function\n\n$$\n(x+2)^{2}Q(x)=(x+2)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{(x+2)^{3}}=\\frac{1}{x+2}\n$$\n\nis *not* analytic about $x=-2$ since its denominator vanishes at $x=-2.$ Therefore, statement II is false.\n\n- Since statement I is false, $x=-2$ is a singular point. Also, since statement II is false, it follows that $x=-2$ is an **irregular** singular point. Thus, statement III is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065872/state.json" "file_sha256" "3d1e52a9f2d0b71be6459449c00f09b0f6cf4f1378677164ef8d0ba4678f2945"} {"question" "q-342307" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065872/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e6600d27b8914742645eb0dde196ab79c45c242c300896e379514617f12f5a0c"} {"question" "q-342307" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065872/state.json" "file_sha256" "3d1e52a9f2d0b71be6459449c00f09b0f6cf4f1378677164ef8d0ba4678f2945"} {"question" "q-342307" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "None"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065872/state.json" "file_sha256" "3d1e52a9f2d0b71be6459449c00f09b0f6cf4f1378677164ef8d0ba4678f2945"} {"question" "q-342307" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065872/state.json" "file_sha256" "3d1e52a9f2d0b71be6459449c00f09b0f6cf4f1378677164ef8d0ba4678f2945"} {"question" "q-342369" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n(x-4){y}^{″}+\\frac{2}{x-4}{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{(x-4)^{2}}y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The functions $P(x)=\\frac{2}{(x-4)^{2}}$ and $Q(x)=\\frac{1}{(x-4)^{3}}$ are analytic about $x=4.$\n- The functions $(x-4)P(x)$ and $(x-4)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=4.$\n- The point $x=4$ is an **irregular** singular point of the differential equation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065872/state.json" "file_sha256" "3d1e52a9f2d0b71be6459449c00f09b0f6cf4f1378677164ef8d0ba4678f2945"} {"question" "q-342369" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A singular point $a$ of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nis regular if both $(x-a)P(x)$ and $(x-a)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, the singular point is irregular.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $(x-4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-4){y}^{″}+\\frac{2}{x-4}{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{(x-4)^{2}}y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{2}{(x-4)^{2}}{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{(x-4)^{3}}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{2}{(x-4)^{2}}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{1}{(x-4)^{3}}.\n$$\n\nLet's now check each statement in turn.\n\n- Since the denominators of both $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ vanish at $x=4,$ they are *not* analytic about $x=4.$ So, statement I is false.\n\n- The function\n\n$$\n(x-4)P(x)=(x-4)\\cdot \\frac{2}{(x-4)^{2}}=\\frac{2}{x-4}\n$$\n\nis *not* analytic about $x=4,$ and the function\n\n$$\n(x-4)^{2}Q(x)=\\frac{1}{x-4}\n$$\n\nis also *not* analytic about $x=4$ since its denominator vanishes at $x=4.$ Therefore, statement II is false.\n\n- Since statement I is false, $x=4$ is a singular point. Also, since statement II is false, it follows that $x=4$ is an **irregular** singular point. Thus, statement III is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065872/state.json" "file_sha256" "3d1e52a9f2d0b71be6459449c00f09b0f6cf4f1378677164ef8d0ba4678f2945"} {"question" "q-342369" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065872/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e6600d27b8914742645eb0dde196ab79c45c242c300896e379514617f12f5a0c"} {"question" "q-342369" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065872/state.json" "file_sha256" "3d1e52a9f2d0b71be6459449c00f09b0f6cf4f1378677164ef8d0ba4678f2945"} {"question" "q-342369" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "None"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065872/state.json" "file_sha256" "3d1e52a9f2d0b71be6459449c00f09b0f6cf4f1378677164ef8d0ba4678f2945"} {"question" "q-342369" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065872/state.json" "file_sha256" "3d1e52a9f2d0b71be6459449c00f09b0f6cf4f1378677164ef8d0ba4678f2945"} {"question" "q-342416" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nx(x-1)(x+3){y}^{″}+(x+3){y}^{′}+x(x-1)y=0\n$$\n\nClassify the following points for the differential equation given above.$\\,$The point $x=2$ is\n{{field-1}}\npoint of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$The point $x=-3$ is\n{{field-2}}\npoint of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$The point $x=0$ is\n{{field-3}}\npoint of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$The point $x=1$ is\n{{field-4}}\npoint of the differential equation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065872/state.json" "file_sha256" "3d1e52a9f2d0b71be6459449c00f09b0f6cf4f1378677164ef8d0ba4678f2945"} {"question" "q-342416" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The point $x=a$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nif both $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, $x=a$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\nA singular point $x=a$ is regular if both $(x-a)P(x)$ and $(x-a)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, the singular point is irregular.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $x(x-1)(x+3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(x-1)(x+3){y}^{″}+(x+3){y}^{′}+x(x-1)y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{x(x-1)}{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{x+3}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{1}{x(x-1)}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{1}{x+3}.\n$$\n\nThe function $P(x)$ is *not* analytic about points where $x(x-1)=0,$ which occurs at $x=0$ and $x=1.$\n\nThe function $Q(x)$ is *not* analytic about points where $x+3=0,$ which occurs at $x=-3.$\n\nTherefore, $x=2$ is $\\text{an ordinary}$ point of the differential equation, and the singular points are\n\n$$\nx=-3,\\,x=0,\\,x=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's classify each singular point.\n\n- For $x=-3,$ the function\n\n$$\n(x+3)P(x)=(x+3)\\cdot \\frac{1}{x(x-1)},\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=-3,$ and the function\n\n$$\n(x+3)^{2}Q(x)=(x+3)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x+3}=x+3,\n$$\n\nis also analytic about $x=-3.$ Therefore, $x=-3$ is $\\text{a regular singular}$ point of the differential equation.\n\n- For $x=0,$ the function\n\n$$\nxP(x)=x\\cdot \\frac{1}{x(x-1)}=\\frac{1}{x-1},\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=0,$ and the function\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}Q(x)={x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x+3},\n$$\n\nis also analytic about $x=0.$ Therefore, $x=0$ is $\\text{a regular singular}$ point of the differential equation.\n\n- For $x=1,$ the function\n\n$$\n(x-1)P(x)=(x-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{x(x-1)}=\\frac{1}{x},\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=1,$ and the function\n\n$$\n(x-1)^{2}Q(x)=(x-1)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x+3},\n$$\n\nis also analytic about $x=1.$ Therefore, $x=1$ is $\\text{a regular singular}$ point of the differential equation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065872/state.json" "file_sha256" "3d1e52a9f2d0b71be6459449c00f09b0f6cf4f1378677164ef8d0ba4678f2945"} {"question" "q-342416" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065872/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e6600d27b8914742645eb0dde196ab79c45c242c300896e379514617f12f5a0c"} {"question" "q-342416" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065872/state.json" "file_sha256" "3d1e52a9f2d0b71be6459449c00f09b0f6cf4f1378677164ef8d0ba4678f2945"} {"question" "q-342416" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "a regular singular"] ["text" "an irregular singular"] ["text" "an ordinary"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065872/state.json" "file_sha256" "3d1e52a9f2d0b71be6459449c00f09b0f6cf4f1378677164ef8d0ba4678f2945"} {"question" "q-342416" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "an ordinary"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065872/state.json" "file_sha256" "3d1e52a9f2d0b71be6459449c00f09b0f6cf4f1378677164ef8d0ba4678f2945"} {"question" "q-34241