n" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065872/state.json" "file_sha256" "3d1e52a9f2d0b71be6459449c00f09b0f6cf4f1378677164ef8d0ba4678f2945"} {"question" "q-342416" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065872/state.json" "file_sha256" "3d1e52a9f2d0b71be6459449c00f09b0f6cf4f1378677164ef8d0ba4678f2945"} {"question" "q-342416" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "a regular singular"] ["text" "an irregular singular"] ["text" "an ordinary"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065872/state.json" "file_sha256" "3d1e52a9f2d0b71be6459449c00f09b0f6cf4f1378677164ef8d0ba4678f2945"} {"question" "q-342416" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "a regular singular"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065872/state.json" "file_sha256" "3d1e52a9f2d0b71be6459449c00f09b0f6cf4f1378677164ef8d0ba4678f2945"} {"question" "q-342642" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nx(x-2)(x+1){y}^{″}+x(x+1){y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}y=0\n$$\n\nClassify the following points for the differential equation given above.$\\,$The point $x=1$ is\n{{field-1}}\npoint of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$The point $x=-1$ is\n{{field-2}}\npoint of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$The point $x=0$ is\n{{field-3}}\npoint of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$The point $x=2$ is\n{{field-4}}\npoint of the differential equation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065872/state.json" "file_sha256" "3d1e52a9f2d0b71be6459449c00f09b0f6cf4f1378677164ef8d0ba4678f2945"} {"question" "q-342642" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The point $x=a$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nif both $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, $x=a$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\nA singular point $x=a$ is regular if both $(x-a)P(x)$ and $(x-a)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, the singular point is irregular.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $x(x-2)(x+1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(x-2)(x+1){y}^{″}+x(x+1){y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{x-2}{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}(x-2)(x+1)}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{1}{x-2}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}(x-2)(x+1)}.\n$$\n\nThe function $P(x)$ is *not* analytic about points where $x-2=0,$ which occurs at $x=2.$\n\nThe function $Q(x)$ is *not* analytic about points where ${x}^{3}(x-2)(x+1)=0,$ which occurs at $x=0,$ $x=2,$ and $x=-1.$\n\nTherefore, $x=1$ is $\\text{an ordinary}$ point of the differential equation, and the singular points are\n\n$$\nx=0,\\,x=2,\\,x=-1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's classify each singular point.\n\n- For $x=0,$ the function\n\n$$\nxP(x)=x\\cdot \\frac{1}{x-2},\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=0,$ while the function\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}Q(x)={x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{3}(x-2)(x+1)}=\\frac{1}{x(x-2)(x+1)},\n$$\n\nis *not* analytic about $x=0.$ Thus, $x=0$ is $\\text{an irregular singular}$ point of the differential equation.\n\n- For $x=2,$ the function\n\n$$\n(x-2)P(x)=(x-2)\\cdot \\frac{1}{x-2}=1,\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=2,$ and the function\n\n$$\n(x-2)^{2}Q(x)=(x-2)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{3}(x-2)(x+1)}=\\frac{x-2}{{x}^{3}(x+1)},\n$$\n\nis also analytic about $x=2.$ Therefore, $x=2$ is $\\text{a regular singular}$ point of the differential equation.\n\n- For $x=-1,$ the function\n\n$$\n(x+1)P(x)=(x+1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{x-2},\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=-1,$ and the function\n\n$$\n(x+1)^{2}Q(x)=(x+1)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{3}(x-2)(x+1)}=\\frac{x+1}{{x}^{3}(x-2)},\n$$\n\nis also analytic about $x=-1.$ Therefore, $x=-1$ is $\\text{a regular singular}$ point of the differential equation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065872/state.json" "file_sha256" "3d1e52a9f2d0b71be6459449c00f09b0f6cf4f1378677164ef8d0ba4678f2945"} {"question" "q-342642" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065872/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e6600d27b8914742645eb0dde196ab79c45c242c300896e379514617f12f5a0c"} {"question" "q-342642" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065872/state.json" "file_sha256" "3d1e52a9f2d0b71be6459449c00f09b0f6cf4f1378677164ef8d0ba4678f2945"} {"question" "q-342642" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "a regular singular"] ["text" "an irregular singular"] ["text" "an ordinary"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065872/state.json" "file_sha256" "3d1e52a9f2d0b71be6459449c00f09b0f6cf4f1378677164ef8d0ba4678f2945"} {"question" "q-342642" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "an ordinary"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065872/state.json" "file_sha256" "3d1e52a9f2d0b71be6459449c00f09b0f6cf4f1378677164ef8d0ba4678f2945"} {"question" 